# tests/probes/continue_in_for_range.vel -- `continue` inside `for i in range(...)`,
# and the C back end's wrong answer to it.
#
# Measured by the leader on 2026-09-20, against `selfhost\build\vm.exe` (the
# compiler that carries libLLVM, 785920 bytes):
#
#     vm.exe run  continue_in_for_range.vel      -> exit 0 in 58 ms, prints "survived"
#     vm.exe build continue_in_for_range.vel
#     continue_in_for_range.exe                  -> STILL RUNNING after 20 seconds,
#                                                   killed; it never prints
#
# So a legal program that the interpreter finishes in 58 ms becomes a program that
# never finishes, and the way a user meets it is `vm.exe build`, which is the
# shipping path.  `parallel for` is refused when it cannot be proved race-free, and
# a program the C back end gets wrong is the same class of promise broken: the
# backend that is *used* must not compile a program into something that does not
# terminate.
#
# Mechanism, from the C the back end emits: `for i in range(a, b)` is lowered to a
# `while` with the increment as the last statement of the body, so C's `continue`
# -- which jumps to the loop's condition, past the rest of the body -- skips the
# increment.  `i` never advances and the loop never ends.  The same program through
# `vm.exe build-llvm` terminates correctly: the LLVM emitter jumps to the increment
# block, which is what the interpreter does.
#
# This is why "build-llvm replaces build" cannot be claimed yet, and it is the one
# concrete counter-example to it: there is a program on which the two back ends
# disagree, and the newer one is the correct one.
#
# The fix belongs in the C emitter (`selfhost/parts/emit.vel`): either put the
# increment in the loop's own `for (...)` clause when the body contains `continue`,
# or lower `continue` to `{ increment; continue; }` in the shapes that have one.
# Whatever is chosen, this file must print "survived" through both back ends, and
# the corpus must stay green.
def main() -> None {
    for i in range(0, 3) {
        continue
    }
    print("survived")
}
